<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The adaptive icon, used from Android 8 onwards.

  The art is the BACKGROUND layer rather than the foreground, and it is baked
  with its own margin rather than relying on the foreground's automatic inset.

  An adaptive icon is composed at 108dp and the launcher then masks it to a
  shape of its choosing. The artwork is a self-contained badge with a border
  running right around it, so anything that crops it eats the border first.

  The size is set by the CIRCLE, because that is the tightest mask in use and
  the one Android's own Settings applies. The badge is a rounded square, so its
  corners reach 1.296 times its half-width from the centre; the circular mask
  cuts at 36dp. Sized to the 72dp viewport the corners landed at 46.7dp, 30%
  outside the circle, and Settings duly showed the border sliced off. 56dp is
  the largest side whose corners stay inside 36dp, so it is what ships: at that
  size no mask shape crops the badge at all.

  The reserved border is filled with the art's own edge colour, which is black
  and therefore invisible against the badge's dark outline.

  It stays the background layer rather than becoming the foreground because the
  foreground would inset it a second time, on top of the inset baked in here.

  The foreground is required and is deliberately empty.

  ic_launcher.png, used by pre-Android 8 launchers and as the round icon, is
  the badge exactly as drawn: rounded corners, transparent outside, no mask to
  survive.
-->
<adaptive-icon xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <background android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_bg" />
    <foreground android:drawable="@android:color/transparent" />
</adaptive-icon>
